            <ArticleTitle>Skull base osteomyelitis following a mastoidectomy.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>584-6</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1016/j.anl.2012.12.002</ELocationID>
            <ELocationID EIdType="pii" ValidYN="Y">S0385-8146(13)00030-8</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Skull base osteomyelitis is a rare but life-threatening condition that is characterized by osteitis of the temporal bone and skull base. Although skull base osteomyelitis is recognized as a rare complication of malignant external otitis or middle ear infection, it may also occur following a mastoidectomy. We present a case of an 81-year-old woman who suffered severe otalgia with normal-looking EAC and tympanic membrane that developed six weeks following a canal wall up mastoidectomy. Otalgia was the only abnormal feature to suspect occurrence of skull base osteomyelitis.</AbstractText>
